The latest flashing instructions & files can be found on [[https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1jJa8LzYnY830v3nBZdOgAk0YQK6OdbSS|Google Drive]].

These devices were “cloud-connected” with automatic upgrades and therefore most devices will be running the latest Cisco firmware.
The Flash Method to use depends on the version of the Bootloader present.
First determine the version of U-Boot on your device:
  - Open the device by unscrewing the 4 screws under the rubber pads with a Torx T5 screwdriver.
  - Connect to the serial console. (Refer to [[#Serial]] and [[docs:techref:hardware:port.serial|Serial Console]] for more info.)
  - Power on the device, the Bootloader version is displayed immediately upon booting.

Please use the table below to find out which flash method to use:

^ Bootloader Version                                                  ^ Flash Method   ^
| [[https://github.com/riptidewave93/meraki-uboot/tree/mr33-20170427|U-Boot 2012.07-g97ab7f1 [local,local] (Oct 06 2016 - 13:07:25)]] | [[#Flash Method A: Replace U-Boot|Replace U-Boot]] (Low complexity) or [[#Flash Method C: Desolder NAND|Desolder NAND]] (High complexity) |
| [[https://github.com/riptidewave93/meraki-uboot/tree/mr33-20190225|U-Boot 2017.07-RELEASE-g78ed34f31579 (Sep 29 2017 - 07:43:44 -0700)]] | [[#Flash Method B: JTAG|JTAG]] (Medium complexity) or [[#Flash Method C: Desolder NAND|Desolder NAND]] (High complexity) |

==== Flash Method A: Replace U-Boot ====

Please verify which Bootloader version you have prior to following these steps. Cisco Meraki APs are known to auto-update without warning.
You risk permanently bricking your device if you try to enter the U-Boot command prompt on a device running any other version then U-Boot 2012.07-g97ab7f1.
If 'Secure boot NOT enabled! Blowing fuses... Resetting now.' is printed on the serial console, it is too late and your device is bricked...

=== Prerequisites ===
  - Download files
    * Modified U-Boot: mr33-uboot.bin from [[https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1jJa8LzYnY830v3nBZdOgAk0YQK6OdbSS]]
    * Serial flasher tool: ubootwrite from [[https://github.com/sebastiaanv3/ubootwrite]]. Make sure you have python3 and pip installed, clone this repository and install the required python dependencies with <code>pip3 install -r requirements.txt</code>
    * Latest initramfs and sysupgrade for the MR33: openwrt-23.05.5-ipq40xx-generic-meraki_mr33-initramfs-uImage.itb and openwrt-23.05.5-ipq40xx-generic-meraki_mr33-squashfs-sysupgrade.bin from [[https://downloads.openwrt.org/releases/23.05.5/targets/ipq40xx/generic/]]
  - Assign your host computer the static IP address 192.168.1.2. The process will vary depending on your operating system.

=== Flashing ===
1. Connect to the Serial Port and Network Interface

2. Load a modified bootloader to RAM memory of the MR33 and boot it. On the host computer run this command, then power up the MR33.
<code>python3 ubootwrite.py --write=mr33-uboot.bin --serial=/dev/ttyUSB0</code> If you get errors you might still need to install the python dependencies.

3. The MR33 will be running a tftp server and waiting for a file transfer. Send it the initramfs file with a tftp client e.g.
<code>echo -e "binary\nput openwrt-23.05.5-ipq40xx-generic-meraki_mr33-initramfs-uImage.itb" | tftp 192.168.1.1</code> or with [[https://github.com/puhitaku/tftp-now|tftp-now]]: <code>tftp-now-linux-amd64 write -host 192.168.1.1 -remote openwrt-23.05.5-ipq40xx-generic-meraki_mr33-initramfs-uImage.itb -local openwrt-23.05.5-ipq40xx-generic-meraki_mr33-initramfs-uImage.itb</code>

4. OpenWrt should boot and you should then be able to SSH into the MR33 with ssh root@192.168.1.1.

5. View available UBI partitions, on the MR33:
<code>ubinfo -a | grep -e "Volume ID" -e "Size" -e "Name"</code>

6. Delete all partitions (except ART as this one contains the calibration data for the wireless NICs), on the MR33:
<code>ubirmvol /dev/ubi0 -n 0
ubirmvol /dev/ubi0 -n 1
ubirmvol /dev/ubi0 -n 2
ubirmvol /dev/ubi0 -n 3
ubirmvol /dev/ubi0 -n 4
ubirmvol /dev/ubi0 -n 5</code>

7. Transfer the initramfs image to the MR33. Unfortunately the latest OpenWrt does not come with the server component required for sftp so the easiest way to do this is to run a http server on your laptop. As you already have python, running <code>python3 -m http.server</code> in the directory containing the two OpenWrt files is an easy way to do this. Then on the MR33 console after changing directory to /tmp:
<code>wget http://192.168.1.2:8000/openwrt-23.05.5-ipq40xx-generic-meraki_mr33-initramfs-uImage.itb</code>
followed by:
<code>wget http://192.168.1.2:8000/openwrt-23.05.5-ipq40xx-generic-meraki_mr33-squashfs-sysupgrade.bin</code>

8. Install the initramfs to the device, on the MR33:
<code>file="/tmp/openwrt-23.05.5-ipq40xx-generic-meraki_mr33-initramfs-uImage.itb"
size=$(cat "$file" | wc -c)
ubimkvol /dev/ubi0 --size=$size --type=static --name=part.old
ubiupdatevol /dev/ubi0_0 "$file"</code>

9. Install the upgrade file, on the MR33:
<code>sysupgrade /tmp/openwrt-23.05.5-ipq40xx-generic-meraki_mr33-squashfs-sysupgrade.bin</code>

